SRC/eff_top -- two changes made for this collection===================================================top.c (2026-09-18).  The process-table walk is bounded by the array as wellas by the count, and printproc is handed the descriptor F$GPrDsc has justread rather than the pointer in the snapshot.WHAT WAS WRONG.  main() takes a snapshot with    anz = _get_process_table ( buffer, BUFFERSIZE<<2 ) ;and then walked `while (i<anz)'.  `anz' comes back larger than the number ofentries the call filled in, and `buffer' holds BUFFERSIZE of them, so theloop ran off the end of the array and read whatever lay beyond it as processpointers.  Measured with two live processes: after the real entry at i=2 itwent on to i=257, 260 and 263, with pointers 30002, af8c2 and 800080, andhanded their `_id' fields to F$GPrDsc.  The bus error that followed -- vector2 at MOVE.W (A0),D0 -- is what every reader of this program saw, a momentafter its column headings.It was NOT the root process's parent, and not the emulator.  The card heresaid so for months, and the card was wrong on both counts.THE CHANGE, three parts:  * `while (i<anz && i<BUFFERSIZE)' -- the array is the real bound.  * `printproc(&procdesc,h)' -- print the descriptor just read.  The    snapshot's pointer can name a process that has since gone.  * printproc checks its argument before following it: the id must index    the tick counters, and the module pointer must be even and carry the    $4AFC sync bytes.  A process whose module has gone is simply not listed.The rebuild is `-qm', so it needs no Microware module: `top' is no longerstarred in DOC/INDEX.  Measured by running it from an execution directoryholding nothing but itself.tools/playtests/top.keys is the test.  Its `absent' lines matter more thanits `expect' lines -- the old binary's crash dump contains the columnheadings, `bash' and `top', so it PASSED the expects; what tells a listingfrom a dump is `Process Aborted'.top.c (2026-09-26).  Which processes it lists.It listed only the processes that had used a tick since its last screen,and `-a' lifted that.  With top alone running it showed its headings andnothing else, or itself only when it happened to catch a tick; an idleshell never appeared.  Now it lists your own processes -- your group.user,from getuid() -- busy or idle, and `-a' lists everyone's.  The usage linesays so.  tools/playtests/topown.keys is the test; the old binary fails it.